OpenStax's own source MathML for the floor-function hint in this exercise
folds two pieces of trailing sentence punctuation into the final inline
math span: <mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo> -- both the
sentence period AND the closing parenthesis of the surrounding "(Hint:
...)" aside are inside the <math> element, not just one trailing token.
trailing_prose_punctuation() in extract-exercises.py only recognizes a
single terminal punctuation token, so it left both characters glued
inside math mode ("$x.)$"), when the closing parenthesis of the Hint
aside should read as ordinary prose immediately after the sentence-ending
period, matching every other parenthetical in this file. This is a
source-authoring quirk (the same one documented for other stray trailing
tokens), not a systematic converter gap -- fixed by hand here.

Manual review also removed redundant outer braces from the two power
bases in the "no c such" exercise block: the converter emitted
$f(x)={(x-1)}^{10}$ and $f(x)={(x-1)}^{9}$, wrapping the already-
parenthesized base in an extra math group. TeX's exponent operator
attaches to the single preceding token/group regardless, so the extra
braces render identically to $f(x)=(x-1)^{10}$ and $f(x)=(x-1)^{9}$;
the braces are dropped as noise, matching the plain parenthesized-base
style used elsewhere in this file.

